        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Taíno</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Lucayan Archipelago (the Bahamas), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, east to Guadeloupe; ceremonial grounds such as Tibes in Puerto Rico.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ceramic Age ancestors entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> c. 2,500 years ago; Taíno <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> met the Spanish in 1492; Spanish documents called them extinct by 1550, but their descendants and revival communities live on today.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacicazgo">cacicazgos</a>) ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a>; matrilineal succession; worship of zemís; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> grown in conuco mounds; the batey ball game; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> hammocks; words such as hammock, canoe, tobacco and hurricane</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Spanish chroniclers (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, Ramón Pané, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, Peter Martyr d’Anghiera); excavated villages, ball courts, zemís and petroglyphs; DNA studies of ancient and living people; oral histories of descendant communities.</span></div>
        </div>
