    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Taíno</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>About 2,500 years ago, the ancestors of the Taíno came to the Caribbean islands from South America.</p><p>The Taíno lived on islands such as Cuba, Jamaica, Hispaniola and Puerto Rico.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cassava.jpg" alt="Cassava" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cassava <span class="kid-tale-cr">David Monniaux, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They grew cassava, a root crop, in heaped-up mounds of earth.</p><p>Women squeezed out its poison and ground it into flour for bread.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotton.jpg" alt="Cotton" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cotton <span class="kid-tale-cr">കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Families lived in large round houses around a village square, led by a chief called a cacique.</p><p>Their homes held cotton hammocks, and the English word hammock comes from their word hamaca.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taino.jpg" alt="Taíno" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Taíno <span class="kid-tale-cr">Tito Caraballo, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They honoured zemís, spirits and ancestors, carved in wood, stone and shell.</p><p>In the village square, they celebrated the deeds of their ancestors with songs.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rubber.jpg" alt="Rubber" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Rubber <span class="kid-tale-cr">CIAT, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They played a ball game called batey, with teams of up to 30 players and a solid rubber ball.</p><p>Games may have helped settle quarrels between villages.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>After Columbus came, the Spanish forced the Taíno to work, and war and disease killed most of them.</p><p>Yet their descendants live on, and thousands of people in Puerto Rico and beyond call themselves Taíno today.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Taíno lived in villages of large round houses around a central plaza, farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> in heaped mounds and were ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a>, whose office usually passed through the female line.</li><li>Their religion centred on zemís, spirits and ancestors given form in carved wood, stone, shell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and honoured with cohoba snuff, songs and ceremonies.</li><li>Spanish conquest after 1492 brought forced labour, war and epidemics that killed most Taíno within decades, yet Taíno descent, customs and identity survive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The English words barbecue, hammock, canoe, tobacco, savanna and hurricane all come from Taíno words: barbacoa, hamaca, kanoa, tabaco, sabana and juracán.</p></aside>
    </section>

